# Chapter 16 — Ship It

A game you cannot share is a game only you will ever play. This last chapter is
about the moment you hand your game to someone else — a friend, a sibling, the
whole world. On the Motoi console that moment has a name: you save your game to a
**cart**, one small file that holds everything, and anyone with a console can load
it and play.

That is what a cart is: your entire game, packed into a single file you can copy,
send, and share. Two verbs do it — `save-cart` writes your game out to a cart, and
`load-cart` reads one back in. Learn those and you can ship.

## Save your game to a cart

Make something on the screen, then pack it into a cart. `save-cart` takes a name
and writes the whole console — your picture and your sound — into a file by that
name:

```motoi
(pset 10 10 12)
(display (if (save-cart \"my-game\") 'saved 'failed))
(newline)
```

```
saved
```

You lit one pixel and called `save-cart \"my-game\"`, and it answered `saved` —
your game is now a file called `my-game` sitting safe on disk. That is the whole
act of shipping in one line. Everything that was on the console at that moment —
every pixel, the audio state, all of it — is now inside that one cart, ready to
travel.

## The cart holds the real picture

Is the game *really* in there, or did `save-cart` just say so? Prove it. Draw a
pixel, save the cart, then *erase* the pixel from the screen — and load the cart
back. If the pixel returns, it came from the cart:

```motoi
(pset 10 10 12)
(save-cart \"my-game\")
(pset 10 10 0)
(load-cart \"my-game\")
(display (pget 10 10))
(newline)
```

```
12
```

Read what happened. You set pixel `(10, 10)` to color `12`, saved, then wiped it
to `0` — gone from the screen. Then `load-cart` brought the cart back, and asking
`(pget 10 10)` returns `12` again. The pixel did not survive on the screen; it
survived *in the cart*. That is proof the cart carries your real game, not just its
name.

## The whole screen travels, not one dot

A cart is not one pixel — it is the *entire* picture. Set two different dots in two
different colors, save, erase both, load, and both come home:

```motoi
(pset 10 10 12)
(pset 20 20 9)
(save-cart \"my-game\")
(pset 10 10 0)
(pset 20 20 0)
(load-cart \"my-game\")
(display (list (pget 10 10) (pget 20 20)))
(newline)
```

```
(12 9)
```

Two dots out, two dots back — `12` and `9`, each its own color, each in its own
place. Whatever your screen holds when you save — a whole level, a title card, a
finished game — the cart holds all of it, and `load-cart` restores every bit. The
cart is a faithful copy of your whole world.

## Hand it to a friend

Because a cart is just a file, you can copy it to anyone. Their console loads it
exactly the way yours does — same picture, same game — and `load-cart` tells them
it worked:

```motoi
(pset 10 10 12)
(save-cart \"my-game\")
(display (if (load-cart \"my-game\") 'loaded 'missing))
(newline)
```

```
loaded
```

`loaded` — the cart opened cleanly and the game is on the screen. This is the
finish line of the whole book: you made a game, and now anyone who loads
`my-game` is *playing* it. Send the file, and you have shipped. A stranger on the
other side of the world can now play something that started as a single pixel you
lit at the prompt.

## What you just proved — and what this whole book proved

You shipped a game. You saved the console into a cart and got `saved`; you proved
the cart carries the real screen by erasing a pixel and loading it back to `12`;
you sent the whole picture through and got both dots home as `(12 9)`; and you
loaded a cart the way a friend would and got `loaded`. Every program ran, and each
one moved your game one step closer to someone else's hands.

And that is the end of the tour. Look back at the road: you booted the console and
found the REPL; you lit the screen, drew a sprite, felt the tick beat; you read
the buttons, moved things with physics, made sound and found the beat; you
animated, collided, built a scene, and assembled a whole playable game; you gave
it music and a memory; and just now you packed it into a cart and shipped it. That
is the entire machine, learned by making things run.

Here is the secret the whole book has been quietly telling you. The console does
not hand you finished games — it hands you a screen, a tick, a rule, a note, a
memory, and a cart, and it trusts *you* to arrange them. The IDE is small; the TUI
is small; they do not have every power a giant game engine has. They do not need
to. With the handful of verbs in this book and an idea of your own, you can make
something real, today — and the next thing you make will be better than the last,
because now the only limit is your imagination and your skill. Those are the two
things no console can hand you, and the two things that make every game worth
playing. Go make one. Then ship it.

> **Tip**
>
> Name your carts so you can find them: `\"pong-v1\"`, `\"maze-level-1\"`,
> `\"birthday-game-for-sam\"`. A cart is a file, so a clear name is how you and
> everyone you share it with will know what is inside before they load it. Keep the
> ones you are proud of; a shelf of carts is a portfolio of everything you have
> made.

> **Warning**
>
> `save-cart` and `load-cart` use a plain *filename*, never a path with slashes —
> the console keeps every cart in its own carts folder on purpose, so a game can
> never reach out and overwrite something else on the machine. If you try to save
> with a name full of slashes or dots, the console cleans it up or refuses. This is
> a safety fence, not a bug: it means a cart you load from a friend can only ever
> touch its own little world, never yours. Appendix F covers the cart format and
> where carts live in full.

